pulse: fall back to the ISP's own analog gain for the day/night reading

The status page asks the streamer for the isp_again metric and hides the
reading when it answers -1. On Ingenic it always answers -1: majestic never
fills its ISP metric store there, and /metrics/isp is empty. So on those
cameras the day/night value simply never appears - and the thresholds it exists
to help choose, minThreshold and maxThreshold, are left to be guessed at.

The number is available: the ISP publishes it in /proc/jz/isp as AeAGain. That
is the same quantity, analog gain, which is what majestic's own help text says
the thresholds are compared against - so a value read here is in the units the
user will type into the settings, not a substitute scale.

Consulted only when the streamer returned nothing or -1, and guarded on
/proc/jz/isp existing, so no other platform changes behaviour or pays for the
read.

Signed-off-by: Oskari Rauta <oskari.rauta@gmail.com>

--- a/www/cgi-bin/j/pulse.cgi
+++ b/www/cgi-bin/j/pulse.cgi
@@ -6,6 +6,19 @@
 	daynight_value=$(wget -q -T1 localhost/metrics/isp?value=isp_again -O -)
 fi
 
+# On Ingenic the streamer never fills its ISP metric store, so isp_again answers
+# -1 and the day/night reading this page is built around never appears at all.
+# The ISP publishes the same quantity itself - analog gain, which is exactly what
+# minThreshold and maxThreshold are documented as being compared against - so the
+# number stays in the units the thresholds are chosen in. Consulted only when the
+# streamer has nothing to say, and guarded on the proc entry existing, so no other
+# platform changes behaviour or pays for the read.
+if [ -z "$daynight_value" ] || [ "$daynight_value" = "-1" ]; then
+	if [ -d /proc/jz/isp ]; then
+		daynight_value=$(awk '/^AeAGain /{print $3; exit}' /proc/jz/isp/* 2>/dev/null)
+	fi
+fi
+
 if [ -n "$temp" ]; then
 	soc_temp="${temp%.*}°C"
 fi
